    "q-70582": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70582",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ and the plane $2x+z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane.",
      "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+z & =1\\,⟹\\,z=1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+((1-2x)-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+(-2x)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
