    "q-71024": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71024",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}+{z}^{2}=8$ and the plane $x-z=2$ onto the $xy$-plane.",
      "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z & =2\\,⟹\\,z=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}+(x-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}+({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+4+(y-5)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x+4+(y-5)^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+2+(y-5)^{2} & =6 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x+1)+(y-5)^{2} & =6 \\\\ 2(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6}=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-71024-a",
