    "q-73251": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73251",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line $x-2=y=\\frac{z}{-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx-2=y=\\frac{z}{-1}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=-t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 3(t+2)^{2}+4(t)^{2}+3(-t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}+4t+4)+4{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}+12t+12 & =12 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}+12t & =0 \\\\ 5{t}^{2}+6t & =0 \\\\ t(5t+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-\\frac{6}{5}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)+2=2 \\\\ y=0 \\\\ z=-(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(2,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{6}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-\\frac{6}{5})+2=\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y=-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ z=-(-\\frac{6}{5})=\\frac{6}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{6}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(2,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{6}{5}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
