    "q-94084": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94084",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=8{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 32{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}+2t+1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t-1 & =0 \\\\ (3t+1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3},1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{3}+1=\\frac{2}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(1)=4 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
